You have already seen these prediction methods in the linear model — quantities of interest from OLS three ways: predictions at means, average effects, and simulation. This page builds them in a detailed way organized around those three methods, and then around ways to measure uncertainty in any of these methods. It is the companion to the nonlinear prediction methods slides; the two are meant to be read together.
Previewing the toolkit in OLS is useful because OLS is the simple case. In the linear model the “link” between the linear prediction \(x\widehat\beta\) and the quantity of interest is the identity function — \(\widehat y = x\widehat\beta\). In the linear model, we don’t have to transform the linear prediction by the link function; the linear prediction is the quantity of interest.

end-point transformation is just \(\widehat y \pm 1.96 \, se(\widehat y)\);
the maximum-likelihood standard error \(\sqrt{\mathrm{diag}(\mathbf{X V X'})}\) is exactly the \(se(\widehat y)\) that predict() produces;
the delta-method standard error of \(\widehat y\) equals that same number;
the confidence interval is symmetric.

Note each of these as we go. In MLE, the link is a nonlinear function that performs important functions: the transformation by \(F\) bounds the interval and bends it, and the two standard errors part ways. But the techniques described below transport directly from the linear case to the nonlinear case.






The process, up front



Every quantity of interest below — and in every model we meet the rest of the semester — comes from the same sequence:

estimate the model;
choose an approach — at-means (hold the other covariates at central tendencies, vary the \(x\) of interest) or average effects (set the \(x\) of interest for the whole estimation sample, predict every case, average);
build the linear prediction \(x\widehat\beta\) for the interesting values of \(x\);
transform through the link (in OLS the link is the identity, so this step does nothing);
attach uncertainty by an analytic standard error, by simulation, or by a bootstrap;
form the interval and plot.

Steps 2 (the approach) and 5 (the technique) are independent choices: any approach pairs with any technique. Keeping them separate organizes everything that follows.

The machine
Here is the whole thing as a single chain. Read the arrows as steps, not as matrix operations: this is the order in which you actually do the work, from a spreadsheet on the left to a quantity of interest on the right. The top line is what you decide. The bottom line is what the machine does once you have decided.
\[
\begin{aligned}
\textbf{choose:}\;&\underbrace{\text{data}}_{\text{spreadsheet}} \;\to\;
\underbrace{X,\; y}_{n \times k,\; n \times 1} \;\to\;
\underbrace{\theta}_{\text{what we want}} \;\to\;
\underbrace{\bbox[#A7DA92, 3pt, border: 1px solid #005A43]{\,f(y \mid \theta)\,}}_{\textbf{swaps}} \;\to\;
\underbrace{\bbox[#A7DA92, 3pt, border: 1px solid #005A43]{\,F\,}}_{\textbf{swaps}} \\[12pt]
\textbf{compute:}\;&\underbrace{X\beta}_{n \times 1} \;\to\;
\underbrace{\widehat\theta = F(X\beta)}_{n \times 1} \;\to\;
\underbrace{\ell(\beta)}_{\text{one number}} \;\to\;
\underbrace{\nabla\ell,\; H}_{k \times 1,\; k \times k} \;\to\;
\underbrace{(-H)^{-1}}_{k \times k} \;\to\;
\underbrace{\text{SE}}_{k \times 1} \;\to\; \text{QI}
\end{aligned}
\]
The two green boxes are the only things that change from one model to the next. Everything else — forming \(X\beta\), the derivatives, the inversion, the square roots — is identical whether we are estimating a logit, a Poisson, or a Cox model. When we move to counts in a few weeks, we swap those two boxes and nothing else moves.
What you choose:

The data are a spreadsheet, \(n\) rows by \(k+1\) columns — one outcome and \(k\) covariates, counting the constant. The first thing estimation does is split it into the design matrix \(X\) (\(n \times k\)) and the outcome \(y\) (\(n \times 1\)).
\(\theta\) is the quantity you want to model — a probability, an expected count, a hazard rate, a time until failure. This is a substantive decision and it comes first. Notice what it does not settle: knowing \(\theta\) is a probability tells you it has to land in \([0,1]\); it tells you nothing about which S-shaped function gets it there.
\(f(y \mid \theta)\) — the first swap — is the distribution that generates \(y\) given that quantity. Look at \(y\) and it is nearly decided for you: a zero or a one gets the Bernoulli, whose parameter is the probability we wanted; a count gets the Poisson, whose parameter is a rate; a duration gets an exponential or a Weibull.
\(F\) — the second swap — is the link, the function mapping the linear predictor onto the space \(\theta\) has to occupy. Knowing \(\theta\) is a probability tells us we need something running from the real line into \([0,1]\), and there are many such things — logit, probit, cloglog are three of them. Choosing among them is a genuinely separate decision, which is why it is a separate step.

What the machine does:

Form the linear predictor \(X\beta\) — an \(n \times k\) matrix times a \(k \times 1\) vector, one number per case. This is the part every model shares with OLS.
Push it through the link for the fitted values \(\widehat\theta = F(X\beta)\), still \(n \times 1\): the model’s estimate of that quantity for every row. (\(\theta\) up top is the quantity we set out to model; \(\widehat\theta\) down here is what a particular \(\beta\) predicts for it.)
Score those fitted values against the observed \(y\) using \(f\), sum the logs, and you have the log-likelihood \(\ell(\beta)\) — one number saying how well this particular \(\beta\) accounts for the whole sample. Note where \(y\) went: it comes in at the far left and then sits out entirely until this step. \(\ell(\beta)\) is the one place in the machine where the data and the model’s predictions are compared.
The gradient, or score, \(\nabla\ell(\beta)\), is \(k \times 1\) — one partial derivative per parameter: a vector, not to be confused with the \(k \times k\) Hessian.
The Hessian \(H\) is \(k \times k\) — the second derivative of \(\ell\) with respect to every pair of parameters. Its negative inverse \((-H)^{-1}\) is the \(k \times k\) variance–covariance matrix, and the square roots of its diagonal are the standard errors.

The score and the Hessian probably mean nothing to you yet; the second half of these notes is about what they are. What to take from the chain now is its shape: two substantive choices, made in the order a researcher actually makes them, then a machine that runs identically every time.

The likelihood notes began to introduce how we produce maximum likelihood estimates:

guess some values for the \(\beta\)s.
evaluate the log-likelihood at those values of \(\beta\).
evaluate the first derivative of the log-likelihood function at that log-likelihood value.
evaluate the second derivative of the function at that log-likelihood value.

So what do we do with these parts to generate estimates? We use those two derivatives to determine how far we are from a maximum and what direction we need to move to go to that maximum. We use that information to make another, more informed guess at the values of \(\beta\), and we do this again.
These notes describe the general algorithm, the Newton-Raphson method. Newton-Raphson is a numerical optimization method originating with Newton’s root finding technique; it iterates over increasingly refined guesses at \(\beta\) until guesses are not producing different, improved estimates.
This allows us to execute the second line of the machine we developed in the likelihood notes:
\[
\begin{aligned}
\textbf{choose:}\;&\underbrace{\text{data}}_{\text{spreadsheet}} \;\to\;
\underbrace{X,\; y}_{n \times k,\; n \times 1} \;\to\;
\underbrace{\theta}_{\text{what we want}} \;\to\;
\underbrace{\bbox[#A7DA92, 3pt, border: 1px solid #005A43]{\,f(y \mid \theta)\,}}_{\textbf{swaps}} \;\to\;
\underbrace{\bbox[#A7DA92, 3pt, border: 1px solid #005A43]{\,F\,}}_{\textbf{swaps}} \\[12pt]
\textbf{compute:}\;&\underbrace{X\beta}_{n \times 1} \;\to\;
\underbrace{\widehat\theta = F(X\beta)}_{n \times 1} \;\to\;
\underbrace{\ell(\beta)}_{\text{one number}} \;\to\;
\underbrace{\nabla\ell,\; H}_{k \times 1,\; k \times k} \;\to\;
\underbrace{(-H)^{-1}}_{k \times k} \;\to\;
\underbrace{\text{SE}}_{k \times 1} \;\to\; \text{QI}
\end{aligned}
\]
You’ll recognize the gradient (or score) \(\nabla\ell\), and the Hessian - the two derivatives from the likelihood notes.
We’ll continue to work with the binomial log-likelihood for familiarity, but recall the generality of the machine. Newton-Raphson operates the same way regardless of the log-likelihood function we use, so it too is incredibly general.
We’ve previously written down a set of steps to build the likelihood based on the data we have. Now, we’ll write a second “recipe” describing the maximization process (started above). This is the process by which we use the data, the model, the log-likelihood, and the two derivatives to generate \(\beta\) and the standard errors.






Maximization using Newton-Raphson




Choose starting values for \(\beta\), call them \(\beta^{(0)}\). Any values will do; all zeros is the usual choice, and it’s the one we use below.
Evaluate the likelihood at those starting values. Form \(X\beta^{(t)}\), push it through the link to get the fitted values, and from those compute the first and second derivatives of the log-likelihood: the score or gradient (which direction is up toward a maximum) and the Hessian (how sharply the surface is curving).
Take a step. Feed the score and the Hessian into the Newton–Raphson update rule, which returns a new set of values, \(\beta^{(t+1)}\).
Check whether we’re done. There are two tests here and it is worth keeping them apart, because they ask different questions. (a) Has the answer stopped moving? Compare \(\beta^{(t+1)}\) with \(\beta^{(t)}\); if the change is effectively zero, another step isn’t buying us anything. (b) Is the surface actually flat here? Evaluate the score at the new values; at a maximum every element of \(\nabla\ell\) should be zero, or near enough. Test (b) is the mathematical condition for a maximum — it is “set the derivative to zero,” checked numerically. If we’re done, those values are \(\widehat\beta\), the maximum likelihood estimates.
If the change isn’t small enough, go back to step 2 with the new values in hand and do it again.
When the loop stops, take the standard errors from the Hessian at the answer: invert it, negate it, and take the square roots of the diagonal.



That list is the map for everything that follows. The rest of this section explains where the rule in step 3 comes from and what its two halves do; then we run steps 1 through 3 once, by hand; then we hand the whole thing to a loop and watch steps 4 and 5 run it to convergence.

Most MLE models are nonlinear, so their coefficients are not their marginal effects. To get a substantive quantity out of one, we have to transform the linear prediction \(x_i\widehat\beta\) through the link. The methods here apply to most MLE applications; the immediate examples are binary-response models, but these slides are the foundation for prediction in every model we encounter this semester.
You’ll be familiar with these methods from the linear-models class — predictions at means, average effects, and simulation-based uncertainty. These slides are a review, but a rigorous one: in the nonlinear world the coefficient really is not the effect, so the transformations do more work. Treat these slides as a reference we’ll refer back to when we need a quantity of interest from a count, a hazard, or a choice model later.






The process, up front



Every quantity of interest described here — and in every model we meet the rest of the semester — comes from the same sequence:

estimate the model;
choose an approach — at-means (hold the other covariates at central tendencies, vary the \(x\) of interest) or average effects (set the \(x\) of interest for the whole estimation sample, predict every case, average);
build the linear prediction \(x\widehat\beta\) for the interesting values of \(x\);
transform through the link, \(F(x\widehat\beta)\) — in the linear model this step did nothing; here it does the work;
attach uncertainty by an analytic standard error, by simulating the parameters, or by a bootstrap;
form the interval and plot.

Steps 2 (the approach) and 5 (the technique) are independent choices: any approach pairs with any technique. These slides are organized around that distinction — the approaches first, then the techniques, then the two joined together to form the confidence interval.

At-means predictions
At-means predictions are what they sound like — effects with the other independent variables held at central tendencies. They are sometimes called adjusted predictions. The recipe:

estimate the model;
create an out-of-sample data frame;
vary the \(x\) of interest; set the other \(x\) variables to appropriate central tendencies (mode for dummies, median for skewed or categorical variables) — hence “at means”;
generate the quantities of interest in that out-of-sample data.

In symbols, the at-means prediction is \(F(\mathbf{x}_0\widehat\beta)\), where \(\mathbf{x}_0\) is a single row we construct — the \(x\) of interest at the value we want, everything else at its central tendency. One row, one prediction. Sweep the \(x\) of interest across its range and we get one prediction per value, and that is the curve we plot. The at-means curve describes one hypothetical case — here, one dyad — and how its probability moves as \(x\) moves.
